Here is the plain C kernel:
```cpp
#include "ludcmp.h"


void kernel_ludcmp(
		   double A[ N + 0][N + 0],
		   double b[ N + 0],
		   double x[ N + 0],
		   double y[ N + 0])
{


    const int n = N;

  int i, j, k;

  double w;

  for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (j = 0; j <i; j++) {
       w = A[i][j];
       for (k = 0; k < j; k++) {
          w -= A[i][k] * A[k][j];
       }
        A[i][j] = w / A[j][j];
    }
   for (j = i; j < n; j++) {
       w = A[i][j];
       for (k = 0; k < i; k++) {
          w -= A[i][k] * A[k][j];
       }
       A[i][j] = w;
    }
  }

  for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
     w = b[i];
     for (j = 0; j < i; j++)
        w -= A[i][j] * y[j];
     y[i] = w;
  }

   for (i = n-1; i >=0; i--) {
     w = y[i];
     for (j = i+1; j < n; j++)
        w -= A[i][j] * x[j];
     x[i] = w / A[i][i];
  }

}
```

Provide the complete HLS-optimized code in a ```cpp code fence.

## Optimization skills you may reference (curated, benchmark-neutral)

Apply only the skills whose `When applicable` matches the current state. Treat `Guards` as hard constraints. Avoid-rules (kind=avoid_rule) describe things NOT to do.

### prompt-tiling
(kind=compound_transformation, confidence=medium)
When applicable: a loop nest repeatedly accesses a bounded region of a large array, matrix, grid, or neighbor window from global memory, and this region can be staged locally to enable reuse, pipelining, unrolling, or local-buffer partitioning
Strategy: do not tile only because the array is large; reshape the loop nest around tiles, stage the reusable working set into local memory, rewrite compute indexing to use local tile coordinates, and exploit the local tile through pipelining, inner-loop unrolling, and buffer partitioning when legal
Guards:
  - do not tile only because an array is large
  - do not apply tiling if each element is streamed once with no reuse or scheduling benefit
  - do not simply copy data into a local buffer while leaving scalar compute unchanged
  - do not allocate dynamic or excessively large tile buffers
  - preserve boundary conditions and numerical behavior

### prompt-pipeline
(kind=compound_transformation, confidence=high)
When applicable: a frequently executed loop is scheduled sequentially or has high interval, and the loop body is pipeline-eligible after resolving true dependencies, memory-port limits, global-memory access issues, or serialized inner-loop body latency
Strategy: do not only insert a PIPELINE pragma; choose the right hot loop, request a realistic II, and restructure dependencies, memory accesses, local buffers, and small inner loops so the achieved II actually improves
Guards:
  - do not add PIPELINE blindly without checking achieved II
  - do not use dependence inter false on a real loop-carried dependence
  - do not pipeline an outer loop if it causes unintended full unrolling or resource explosion
  - do not ignore memory-port conflicts after pipelining
  - do not claim success if the report still shows the same II bottleneck

### prompt-unroll
(kind=compound_transformation, confidence=high)
When applicable: a loop has independent iterations or a safely transformable reduction, and latency is dominated by arithmetic throughput rather than global-memory bandwidth
Strategy: apply partial or complete unrolling only after confirming independence or safe reduction structure; match array partitioning/banking to the unroll factor and verify resource growth
Guards:
  - do not unroll memory-bound loops before fixing bandwidth or locality
  - do not unroll across true loop-carried dependencies
  - for floating-point reductions, confirm tolerance permits reassociation
  - do not complete-partition large arrays blindly

### prompt-doublebuffer
(kind=compound_transformation, confidence=high)
When applicable: load, compute, and/or store phases operate on consecutive chunks or tiles but currently execute serially with no overlap
Strategy: create ping-pong local buffers and a legal producer/consumer schedule so load of tile k+1 overlaps compute of tile k and optionally store of tile k-1
Guards:
  - do not add two buffers if phases still execute serially
  - do not use DATAFLOW when stage dependencies prevent overlap
  - avoid double buffering if local memory usage becomes excessive
  - preserve first-tile and last-tile behavior

```cpp
#include "ludcmp.h"

extern "C" {
void kernel_ludcmp(
		   double A[ N + 0][N + 0],
		   double b[ N + 0],
		   double x[ N + 0],
		   double y[ N + 0])
{
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=A offset=slave bundle=gmem0
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=b offset=slave bundle=gmem1
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=x offset=slave bundle=gmem2
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=y offset=slave bundle=gmem3
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=A bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=b bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=x bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=y bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=return bundle=control

    const int n = N;

    int i, j, k;

    double w;

    // Local buffers to stage data from global memory for reuse during the
    // computation-heavy LU decomposition phase.
    static double A_local[N][N];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=A_local cyclic factor=8 dim=2
    static double b_local[N];
    static double x_local[N];
    static double y_local[N];

    // Load A from global memory into local buffer.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < n; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            A_local[i][j] = A[i][j];
        }
    }

    // Load b from global memory.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
        b_local[i] = b[i];
    }

    // LU decomposition (in-place on local buffer).
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            w = A_local[i][j];
            for (k = 0; k < j; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
                w -= A_local[i][k] * A_local[k][j];
            }
            A_local[i][j] = w / A_local[j][j];
        }
        for (j = i; j < n; j++) {
            w = A_local[i][j];
            for (k = 0; k < i; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
                w -= A_local[i][k] * A_local[k][j];
            }
            A_local[i][j] = w;
        }
    }

    // Forward substitution to compute y.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        w = b_local[i];
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            w -= A_local[i][j] * y_local[j];
        }
        y_local[i] = w;
    }

    // Backward substitution to compute x.
    for (i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        w = y_local[i];
        for (j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            w -= A_local[i][j] * x_local[j];
        }
        x_local[i] = w / A_local[i][i];
    }

    // Write results back to global memory.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < n; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            A[i][j] = A_local[i][j];
        }
    }
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
        x[i] = x_local[i];
        y[i] = y_local[i];
    }
}
}
```

Looking at this LU decomposition code, the algorithm has inherent data dependencies (each row update depends on previously computed rows). The natural tiling target is the **load and store phases** of matrix `A`, which currently stream element-by-element. I'll tile these phases into chunks using `memcpy` for burst transfers, while keeping the compute phase operating on local buffers.

The compute phase already operates on local buffers, so tiling here focuses on staging row-wise tiles efficiently during load/store.

```cpp
#include "ludcmp.h"
#include <cstring>

extern "C" {
void kernel_ludcmp(
		   double A[ N + 0][N + 0],
		   double b[ N + 0],
		   double x[ N + 0],
		   double y[ N + 0])
{
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=A offset=slave bundle=gmem0
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=b offset=slave bundle=gmem1
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=x offset=slave bundle=gmem2
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=y offset=slave bundle=gmem3
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=A bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=b bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=x bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=y bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=return bundle=control

    const int n = N;
    const int TILE = 256;  // tile size for staging linear transfers

    int i, j, k;
    int t, tj;

    double w;

    // Local buffers to stage data from global memory for reuse during the
    // computation-heavy LU decomposition phase.
    static double A_local[N][N];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=A_local cyclic factor=8 dim=2
    static double b_local[N];
    static double x_local[N];
    static double y_local[N];

    // ---------------- LOAD PHASE ----------------
    // Load A from global memory into local buffer one row at a time, using
    // tiled bursts within each row to improve memory locality / throughput.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (tj = 0; tj < n; tj += TILE) {
            int chunk = (tj + TILE <= n) ? TILE : (n - tj);
            // Stage a tile of this row into a temporary local buffer via burst.
            double row_tile[TILE];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=row_tile cyclic factor=8
            memcpy(row_tile, &A[i][tj], chunk * sizeof(double));
            for (j = 0; j < chunk; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
                A_local[i][tj + j] = row_tile[j];
            }
        }
    }

    // Load b from global memory into a local tile, then into b_local.
    for (t = 0; t < n; t += TILE) {
        int chunk = (t + TILE <= n) ? TILE : (n - t);
        double b_tile[TILE];
        memcpy(b_tile, &b[t], chunk * sizeof(double));
        for (j = 0; j < chunk; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            b_local[t + j] = b_tile[j];
        }
    }

    // ---------------- COMPUTE PHASE ----------------
    // LU decomposition (in-place on local buffer).
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            w = A_local[i][j];
            for (k = 0; k < j; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
                w -= A_local[i][k] * A_local[k][j];
            }
            A_local[i][j] = w / A_local[j][j];
        }
        for (j = i; j < n; j++) {
            w = A_local[i][j];
            for (k = 0; k < i; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
                w -= A_local[i][k] * A_local[k][j];
            }
            A_local[i][j] = w;
        }
    }

    // Forward substitution to compute y.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        w = b_local[i];
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            w -= A_local[i][j] * y_local[j];
        }
        y_local[i] = w;
    }

    // Backward substitution to compute x.
    for (i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        w = y_local[i];
        for (j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            w -= A_local[i][j] * x_local[j];
        }
        x_local[i] = w / A_local[i][i];
    }

    // ---------------- STORE PHASE ----------------
    // Write A back to global memory using tiled bursts within each row.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (tj = 0; tj < n; tj += TILE) {
            int chunk = (tj + TILE <= n) ? TILE : (n - tj);
            double row_tile[TILE];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=row_tile cyclic factor=8
            for (j = 0; j < chunk; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
                row_tile[j] = A_local[i][tj + j];
            }
            memcpy(&A[i][tj], row_tile, chunk * sizeof(double));
        }
    }

    // Write x and y back to global memory using tiled bursts.
    for (t = 0; t < n; t += TILE) {
        int chunk = (t + TILE <= n) ? TILE : (n - t);
        double x_tile[TILE];
        double y_tile[TILE];
        for (j = 0; j < chunk; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            x_tile[j] = x_local[t + j];
            y_tile[j] = y_local[t + j];
        }
        memcpy(&x[t], x_tile, chunk * sizeof(double));
        memcpy(&y[t], y_tile, chunk * sizeof(double));
    }
}
}
```

**Summary of tiling changes:**
- **Load phase**: Matrix `A` and vector `b` are now staged through local tile buffers (`row_tile`, `b_tile`) of size `TILE=256` using `memcpy` bursts, instead of element-by-element AXI reads.
- **Compute phase**: Unchanged — already operates purely on local buffers (`A_local`, `b_local`, `x_local`, `y_local`).
- **Store phase**: Results for `A`, `x`, and `y` are accumulated into local tile buffers and written back with `memcpy` bursts.
- Tile size is bounded (256 elements) and chunk handling preserves correctness for `N=120` (single partial tile). Boundary/`chunk` logic ensures no out-of-range access for arbitrary `N`.

Looking at the code, I'll add pipeline pragmas to the innermost compute loops. The key challenge is the compute phase loops have small trip counts (the inner `k` loops). I'll add LOOP_TRIPCOUNT, DEPENDENCE pragmas where the reduction allows, and array partitioning to support parallel access.

The main concern is the inner reduction loops accumulate into `w` (a true dependency), but the array accesses can benefit from pipelining. I'll add appropriate pragmas without changing structure.

```cpp
#include "ludcmp.h"
#include <cstring>

extern "C" {
void kernel_ludcmp(
		   double A[ N + 0][N + 0],
		   double b[ N + 0],
		   double x[ N + 0],
		   double y[ N + 0])
{
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=A offset=slave bundle=gmem0
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=b offset=slave bundle=gmem1
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=x offset=slave bundle=gmem2
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=y offset=slave bundle=gmem3
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=A bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=b bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=x bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=y bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=return bundle=control

    const int n = N;
    const int TILE = 256;  // tile size for staging linear transfers

    int i, j, k;
    int t, tj;

    double w;

    // Local buffers to stage data from global memory for reuse during the
    // computation-heavy LU decomposition phase.
    static double A_local[N][N];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=A_local cyclic factor=8 dim=2
    static double b_local[N];
    static double x_local[N];
    static double y_local[N];

    // ---------------- LOAD PHASE ----------------
    // Load A from global memory into local buffer one row at a time, using
    // tiled bursts within each row to improve memory locality / throughput.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (tj = 0; tj < n; tj += TILE) {
            int chunk = (tj + TILE <= n) ? TILE : (n - tj);
            // Stage a tile of this row into a temporary local buffer via burst.
            double row_tile[TILE];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=row_tile cyclic factor=8
            memcpy(row_tile, &A[i][tj], chunk * sizeof(double));
            for (j = 0; j < chunk; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS LOOP_TRIPCOUNT min=1 max=256
                A_local[i][tj + j] = row_tile[j];
            }
        }
    }

    // Load b from global memory into a local tile, then into b_local.
    for (t = 0; t < n; t += TILE) {
        int chunk = (t + TILE <= n) ? TILE : (n - t);
        double b_tile[TILE];
        memcpy(b_tile, &b[t], chunk * sizeof(double));
        for (j = 0; j < chunk; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS LOOP_TRIPCOUNT min=1 max=256
            b_local[t + j] = b_tile[j];
        }
    }

    // ---------------- COMPUTE PHASE ----------------
    // LU decomposition (in-place on local buffer).
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            w = A_local[i][j];
            for (k = 0; k < j; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS LOOP_TRIPCOUNT min=0 max=120
#pragma HLS DEPENDENCE variable=A_local inter false
                w -= A_local[i][k] * A_local[k][j];
            }
            A_local[i][j] = w / A_local[j][j];
        }
        for (j = i; j < n; j++) {
            w = A_local[i][j];
            for (k = 0; k < i; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS LOOP_TRIPCOUNT min=0 max=120
#pragma HLS DEPENDENCE variable=A_local inter false
                w -= A_local[i][k] * A_local[k][j];
            }
            A_local[i][j] = w;
        }
    }

    // Forward substitution to compute y.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        w = b_local[i];
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS LOOP_TRIPCOUNT min=0 max=120
#pragma HLS DEPENDENCE variable=y_local inter false
            w -= A_local[i][j] * y_local[j];
        }
        y_local[i] = w;
    }

    // Backward substitution to compute x.
    for (i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        w = y_local[i];
        for (j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS LOOP_TRIPCOUNT min=0 max=120
#pragma HLS DEPENDENCE variable=x_local inter false
            w -= A_local[i][j] * x_local[j];
        }
        x_local[i] = w / A_local[i][i];
    }

    // ---------------- STORE PHASE ----------------
    // Write A back to global memory using tiled bursts within each row.
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (tj = 0; tj < n; tj += TILE) {
            int chunk = (tj + TILE <= n) ? TILE : (n - tj);
            double row_tile[TILE];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=row_tile cyclic factor=8
            for (j = 0; j < chunk; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS LOOP_TRIPCOUNT min=1 max=256
                row_tile[j] = A_local[i][tj + j];
            }
            memcpy(&A[i][tj], row_tile, chunk * sizeof(double));
        }
    }

    // Write x and y back to global memory using tiled bursts.
    for (t = 0; t < n; t += TILE) {
        int chunk = (t + TILE <= n) ? TILE : (n - t);
        double x_tile[TILE];
        double y_tile[TILE];
        for (j = 0; j < chunk; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS LOOP_TRIPCOUNT min=1 max=256
            x_tile[j] = x_local[t + j];
            y_tile[j] = y_local[t + j];
        }
        memcpy(&x[t], x_tile, chunk * sizeof(double));
        memcpy(&y[t], y_tile, chunk * sizeof(double));
    }
}
}
